Modify Your Live App Using AI (Safely)
Video 2 Script (teleprompter-ready, one sentence per line)

Today I’m going to modify the live app we deployed in video 1.
But this time we do it locally.
And we do it with AI.
Safely.
Controlled.
No chaos.

The outcome you want is simple.
AI becomes a power tool.
Not a random code generator.

We are using the same project as before.
So nothing new to set up.
We just extend what already exists.

Here is the flow for today.
Open the repo locally.
Open it in Antigravity.
Add a new page.
Add a simple API route.
Commit.
Push.
And watch Vercel redeploy automatically.

Before we touch anything, one rule.
We only change one thing at a time.
Small steps.
Small commits.
Easy rollbacks.

Step one.
Clone the repo to your computer.
You can do this with Antigravity’s clone feature.
Or you can do it with Git in your terminal.

Once it’s local, open the project in Antigravity.
On the left you have files.
On the right you have AI.
That’s the whole game.

Now we set guardrails.
We tell the AI exactly what we want.
We tell it which files it may touch.
We tell it to keep changes minimal.
And we tell it to show the diff before we accept anything.

Here is the prompt I’m going to use.
Add a new page called /ai-check.
Keep styling consistent with the existing app.
Add one button that calls an API route.
And display the API response on the page.
Do not refactor anything else.
Do not touch unrelated files.
Show the final diff.

Now I run it.
I review what it changed.
I’m not trusting it blindly.
I’m checking the exact file edits.

If the AI changed extra things, I reject it.
If the AI touched unrelated files, I reject it.
If the AI made the diff too big, I reject it.

Assuming it’s clean, I accept the change.

Now let’s add the API route.
We keep it boring on purpose.
A simple endpoint like /api/ping.
It returns JSON.
Something like ok true and a timestamp.

Again, I prompt the AI with constraints.
Create /api/ping that returns a small JSON response.
Do not add dependencies.
Do not edit other API routes.
Show the diff.

I run it.
I review it.
I accept it only if it’s minimal and correct.

Now we test locally.
We start the dev server.
We open the new page.
We click the button.
We confirm we see the API response.

If anything breaks, we stop.
We fix the smallest thing.
Then we re-test.

Now we commit.
One commit for the new page and API route.
A clear message.
Add ai-check page and ping API route.

Now we push to GitHub.
This is the trigger.
GitHub updates.
Vercel sees it.
Vercel redeploys automatically.

Now we verify in production.
Open the live URL.
Visit /ai-check.
Click the button.
Confirm the API response is working live.

And that’s the point of this video.
AI didn’t replace your brain.
It amplified your workflow.
Because you constrained it.
You reviewed it.
And you shipped it in small steps.

AI is controlled.
Not chaotic.

In the next video, we’ll cover what usually breaks in this setup.
And the simple checks that prevent pain later.